WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.625
Two federal documents frame
the reset and recovery path.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:09.161
OMB's zero trust memorandum, M-22-09,
requires phishing-resistant M F A,

3
00:00:09.161 --> 00:00:13.870
and then names the hole: recovery
processes are a potential bypass of

4
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:19.075
standard authentication, and a significant
threat vector if not mitigated.

5
00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:23.223
It asks for high-friction methods
that are costly for an adversary,

6
00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:26.013
such as in-person
verification or live video.

7
00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:28.835
NIST's digital identity guidelines,

8
00:00:28.835 --> 00:00:32.787
revised in July 2025, give
recovery its own section.

9
00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:36.180
They recognize four methods:
saved recovery codes,

10
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.188
issued recovery codes, recovery
contacts, and repeated identity proofing.

11
00:00:41.188 --> 00:00:45.818
A help desk agent is allowed only on
the basis of a documented risk analysis,

12
00:00:45.818 --> 00:00:49.402
repeated proofing has to be
consistent with the original,

13
00:00:49.402 --> 00:00:52.538
and every recovery must
notify the account holder.

14
00:00:52.538 --> 00:00:57.137
Read together, the help desk reset has
to be an exception, not the main path;

15
00:00:57.137 --> 00:00:59.436
high-friction for the account's risk;

16
00:00:59.436 --> 00:01:04.188
at least as strong as the original
proofing; and always followed by a notice.

17
00:01:04.188 --> 00:01:08.020
Email, caller I D and knowledge
questions verify no one.
